      "math_academy_id": "e-980",
      "problem": "A solution to the linear equation $y-5x=2$ is the ordered pair $(2,k).$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $(2,k)$ is a solution to the linear equation $y-5x=2,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $2$ and $k$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5x & =2 \\\\ (k)-5(2) & =2 \\\\ k-10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-10 & =2 \\\\ k & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
